  "Question": "The discriminant of the equation \\(x^2+2x\\sqrt3+3=0\\) is zero. Hence, its roots are:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "real and equal",
    "B": "rational and equal",
    "C": "rational and unequal",
    "D": "irrational and unequal",
    "E": "imaginary"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) is the quantity under the square root in the quadratic formula. When \\(\\Delta=0\\), the \\(+\\sqrt\\Delta\\) and \\(-\\sqrt\\Delta\\) terms are both zero, so the two roots coincide.\n\nHere the quadratic coefficient is \\(1\\), the linear coefficient is \\(2\\sqrt3\\), and the given discriminant is zero. Therefore\n\\[\nx=\\frac{-2\\sqrt3\\pm\\sqrt0}{2}=-\\sqrt3.\n\\]\nBoth roots are the same real number, so they are real and equal, choice A.",
